<edge source="china_tech_trade_rivalry::regulatory_scrutiny" target="global_economic_strain::cross_border_capital_flo" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.8</data>
  <data key="d36">Heightened regulatory scrutiny (e.g., tighter foreign-ownership limits, inspections, or delisting risk) prompts foreign investors to reduce or withdraw allocations to Chinese tech, directly cutting cross-border capital flows into the market.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">china_tech_trade_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d39">global_economic_strain</data>
</edge>
<edge source="china_tech_trade_rivalry::regulatory_scrutiny" target="global_economic_strain::market_volatility" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.85</data>
  <data key="d36">New or intensified regulatory actions increase uncertainty about earnings, compliance costs and legal risk for large tech firms, triggering abrupt repricing and spikes in equity and options-implied volatility across markets.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">china_tech_trade_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d39">global_economic_strain</data>
</edge>
<edge source="china_tech_trade_rivalry::regulatory_scrutiny" target="global_energy_supply_cri::maritime_attack_rate" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">Escalating regulatory scrutiny and export controls against Chinese tech firms provoke state-led or state-tolerated asymmetric retaliation and coercive maritime operations (harassment, interceptions, attacks on vessels in contested sea lanes) as a political signal, raising the incidence of hostile actions against commercial shipping and maritime energy infrastructure.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">china_tech_trade_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d39">global_energy_supply_cri</data>
</edge>
<edge source="china_tech_trade_rivalry::regulatory_scrutiny" target="iran_proxy_escalation::supply_chain_disruption" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">Stricter Chinese export controls and enforcement on dual‑use chips, sensors and AI-enabled subsystems immediately reduce legally available channels for proxies to obtain strike-enabling hardware, degrading their ability to interdict shipping and regional logistics and thereby lowering the incidence of attack-driven supply‑chain disruption.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">china_tech_trade_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d39">iran_proxy_escalation</data>
</edge>
<edge source="china_tech_trade_rivalry::regulatory_scrutiny" target="russia_ukraine_war::sanctions_enforcement" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">Tighter Chinese regulatory scrutiny that expands export controls and compliance checks makes Chinese firms and banks less willing/able to route sanctioned goods and transactions to Russian actors, increasing the effective intensity of sanctions enforcement.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">china_tech_trade_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
